\begin{abstract}
Electric vehicle (EV) charging infrastructure information is often distributed across fragmented data sources with disparate structures, making it difficult to answer practical queries such as ``which stations offer DC fast charging above $100\text{ kW}$ for a specific vehicle model?'' or ``which operators manage these locations?''. We propose the PluggedIn ontology, a lightweight OWL 2 ontology describing charging stations, ports, connectors, vehicles, and operators. PluggedIn reuses established vocabularies including FOAF, WGS84 Geo, and OWL-Time. We demonstrate how new charging stations and instances are added to the knowledge graph, how triples are updated, and how complex multi-hop competency questions are answered using SPARQL 1.1 queries and reasoner inferences over an OpenLink Virtuoso triplestore. The codebase, installation instructions, and software artifacts are available at \url{https://github.com/chohoon901/PluggedIn}.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{Semantic Web, Ontology, OWL 2, SPARQL, Knowledge Graph, EV Charging Infrastructure}

\maketitle

% ===============================================================
\section{Problem Definition}
% ===============================================================
Our goal is to build a comprehensive knowledge graph of electric vehicle charging infrastructure and accessibility in British Columbia. Existing data sources store station records with network names as flat strings and operating hours as free text, making it difficult for automated systems or drivers to check cross-network compatibility, port power thresholds, or adapter requirements.

We want a shared, machine-readable model so that drivers, fleet operators, and web applications can query integrated charging data seamlessly. Formally, given a set of station records $R=\{r_{1},...,r_{n}\}$, we want to build an RDF graph $G$ of triples $(s, p, o)$ governed by an ontology $O$, such that the following competency questions (CQs) can be answered via SPARQL queries over $G$:
\begin{itemize}
    \setlength\itemsep{0pt}
    \item \textbf{CQ1:} Which stations are operated by a specific network (e.g., BC Hydro EV Network), and what are their geographic coordinates?
    \item \textbf{CQ2:} Which stations offer DC fast charging ports above $100\text{ kW}$, and what connector types do they use?
    \item \textbf{CQ3:} Which registered vehicles are compatible with specific connector types available at local stations?
    \item \textbf{CQ4:} Which stations require specific adapter types to bridge incompatible vehicle inlets and charging ports?
\end{itemize}

% ===============================================================
\section{Proposed Ontology (TBox)}
% ===============================================================
We build the PluggedIn ontology in Protégé \cite{musen2015} using OWL 2 \cite{owl2} and store it in Turtle syntax within the named graph \texttt{https://github.com/chohoon901/PluggedIn/graph/}. Instead of defining every domain concept from scratch, we reuse existing Semantic Web vocabularies (Table~\ref{tab:namespaces}). An infrastructure entity inherits properties from standard foundational ontologies. Figure~\ref{fig:class_hierarchy} illustrates the complete asserted class hierarchy covering our 13 core domain entities and organizational subclasses.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Namespaces used in the PluggedIn Ontology.}
\label{tab:namespaces}
\begin{tabular}{ll}
\toprule
\textbf{Prefix} & \textbf{Vocabulary / Namespace URI} \\
\midrule
\texttt{plug:} & Our ontology, \url{https://github.com/chohoon901/PluggedIn/ontology\#} \\
\texttt{event:} & Event Ontology \cite{event} \\
\texttt{time:} & OWL-Time \cite{time} \\
\texttt{foaf:} & Friend of a Friend \cite{foaf} \\
\texttt{geo:} & W3C WGS84 Geo Positioning \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

