\subsection{First Main Entity Set: Station, Port, and Hardware}
The core class of our ontology is \texttt{plug:Station}, defined as a physical facility providing electric vehicle supply equipment (EVSE). Subclasses and associated hardware include \texttt{plug:Port}, \texttt{plug:ConnectorType}, and \texttt{plug:Adapter}. Datatype properties such as \texttt{plug:powerKw} (functional, in kilowatts), \texttt{plug:currentType} (AC or DC), and \texttt{plug:portNumber} characterize individual ports.

To handle real-world interoperability, we model adapter-mediated compatibility explicitly. An \texttt{plug:Adapter} connects two connector standards via \texttt{plug:fromConnector} and \texttt{plug:toConnector}, with a \texttt{plug:connectorMaxPowerKw} constraint. Listing 1 shows an excerpt of the TBox definitions for this primary hardware set.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=sparql, caption={Definition of core station and hardware classes and properties (excerpt).}]
plug:Station a owl:Class .
plug:Port a owl:Class .
plug:ConnectorType a owl:Class .
plug:Adapter a owl:Class .

plug:hasPort a owl:ObjectProperty ;
  rdfs:domain plug:Station ;
  rdfs:range plug:Port .

plug:hasConnectorType a owl:ObjectProperty ;
  rdfs:domain plug:Port ;
  rdfs:range plug:ConnectorType .

plug:HighPowerDCStation a owl:Class ;
  owl:equivalentClass [ a owl:Restriction ;
    owl:onProperty plug:hasPort ;
    owl:someValuesFrom [ a owl:Restriction ;
      owl:onProperty plug:powerKw ;
      owl:minInclusive "100.0"^^xsd:decimal ] ] .
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{Second Main Entity Set: Operator, Network, and Driver}
The second main entity set models organizational actors and users. \texttt{plug:Organization} serves as a generalized superclass with specialized subclasses: \texttt{plug:Network}, \texttt{plug:Operator}, and \texttt{plug:SiteOwner}. A station is linked to its network operator via \texttt{plug:isOperatedBy} (a sub-property of organizational association). 

Furthermore, drivers and their network memberships are modeled via \texttt{plug:Driver} and \texttt{plug:Membership}, connecting users to networks to determine payment eligibility. Descriptive attributes like \texttt{plug:operatorName} and \texttt{plug:paymentMethod} capture operational details.

\subsection{Third Main Entity Set: Location and Time}
Because charging stations exist physically and operate on schedules, the third main entity set models spatial coordinates and temporal constraints. \texttt{plug:Location} is a subclass of \texttt{geo:SpatialThing}, providing an \texttt{rdfs:label}, \texttt{geo:lat}, and \texttt{geo:long} for map rendering. Operating schedules are separated into \texttt{plug:OperatingPeriod} entities with properties for days and hours, avoiding ambiguous free text. Time instants utilize \texttt{time:Instant} from OWL-Time \cite{time}. Table~\ref{tab:properties} summarizes the primary object and datatype properties across these entity sets.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\caption{Main PluggedIn properties (O: object property, D: datatype property).}
\label{tab:properties}
\begin{tabular}{llll}
\toprule
\textbf{Property} & \textbf{Type} & \textbf{Domain} & \textbf{Range} \\
\midrule
\texttt{isOperatedBy} & O & Station & Operator \\
\texttt{hasPort} & O & Station & Port \\
\texttt{hasConnectorType} & O & Port & ConnectorType \\
\texttt{vehicleAccepts} & O & Vehicle & ConnectorType \\
\texttt{powerKw} & D & Port & xsd:decimal \\
\texttt{stationName} & D & Station & xsd:string \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

% ===============================================================
\section{Main SPARQL Statements}
% ===============================================================
All statements conform to SPARQL 1.1 \cite{harris2013} and were executed on our Virtuoso knowledge graph using standard prefixes:
\begin{lstlisting}[language=sparql]
PREFIX rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>
PREFIX xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#>
PREFIX time: <http://www.w3.org/2006/time#>
PREFIX plug: <https://github.com/chohoon901/PluggedIn/ontology#>
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{New Triples and ABox Population}
When new station records arrive, triples are inserted via \texttt{INSERT DATA}. Listing 3 populates ABox instances representing Victoria charging infrastructure into the named graph.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=sparql, caption={Adding a new charging station instance (Listing 3).}]
INSERT DATA {
  GRAPH <https://github.com/chohoon901/PluggedIn/graph/> {
    plug:operatorBCHydro a plug:Operator ;
      plug:operatorName "BC Hydro EV Network" .
    plug:station001 a plug:Station ;
      plug:stationName "Uptown EV Hub" ;
      plug:latitude "48.4500"^^xsd:decimal ;
      plug:longitude "-123.3800"^^xsd:decimal ;
      plug:isOperatedBy plug:operatorBCHydro .
  }
}
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\columnwidth]{ABox Population&Execution_Result.png}
\caption{Virtuoso ISQL execution confirmation for ABox triple insertion.}
\label{fig:abox_result}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Updates to Triples}
SPARQL updates combine \texttt{DELETE} and \texttt{INSERT} blocks. Listing 4 updates an operator name or network association across matching station instances.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=sparql, caption={Updating station network associations (Listing 4).}]
DELETE { ?s plug:isOperatedBy plug:oldOp }
INSERT { ?s plug:isOperatedBy plug:newOp }
WHERE  { ?s plug:isOperatedBy plug:oldOp .
         ?oldOp plug:operatorName "Old Network Name" }
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{Querying the Knowledge Graph}
We answer each competency question with a SPARQL \texttt{SELECT} query. Listing 6 answers CQ1, retrieving stations operated by the BC Hydro EV Network along with their coordinates.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=sparql, caption={CQ1: Stations and coordinates by operator (Listing 6).}]
SELECT ?stationName ?opName ?lat ?lon WHERE {
  GRAPH <https://github.com/chohoon901/PluggedIn/graph/> {
    ?station a plug:Station ;
             plug:stationName ?stationName ;
             plug:latitude ?lat ;
             plug:longitude ?lon ;
             plug:isOperatedBy ?op .
    ?op plug:operatorName ?opName .
    FILTER (?opName = "BC Hydro EV Network")
  }
} ORDER BY ?stationName
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\columnwidth]{CQ1_Result.png}
\caption{Result of CQ1 query executed in Virtuoso ISQL.}
\label{fig:cq1_result}
\end{figure}

Listings 7 and 8 utilize aggregation functions to analyze station density and connector distribution across regions.

\begin{lstlisting}[language=sparql, caption={CQ2: Number of high-power ports per station (Listing 7).}]
SELECT ?stationName (COUNT(?port) AS ?dcPorts) WHERE {
  GRAPH <https://github.com/chohoon901/PluggedIn/graph/> {
    ?station plug:stationName ?stationName ;
             plug:hasPort ?port .
    ?port plug:currentType "DC" .
  }
} GROUP BY ?stationName ORDER BY DESC(?dcPorts)
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{lstlisting}[language=sparql, caption={CQ3: Vehicle and connector compatibility (Listing 8).}]
SELECT ?vehicleModel ?connName WHERE {
  GRAPH <https://github.com/chohoon901/PluggedIn/graph/> {
    ?v a plug:Vehicle ;
       plug:vehicleModel ?vehicleModel ;
       plug:vehicleAccepts ?conn .
    ?conn plug:connectorName ?connName .
  }
} ORDER BY ?vehicleModel
\end{lstlisting}

\begin{figure}[h]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\columnwidth]{CQ3_Result.png}
\caption{Result of CQ3 query matching vehicles to connector types.}
\label{fig:cq3_result}
\end{figure}

Reasoning engines can also evaluate class expressions automatically; with an OWL 2 reasoner enabled, querying instances of \texttt{plug:HighPowerDCStation} identifies stations satisfying power thresholds without manual filter clauses.

% ===============================================================
\section{Conclusion}
% ===============================================================
Migrating the PluggedIn data model to an OWL 2 knowledge graph hosted on an OpenLink Virtuoso triplestore demonstrates significant advantages over traditional relational storage. By modeling chargers, connectors, operators, and vehicles as interconnected RDF triples, complex multi-hop queries regarding power capacities, network memberships, and vehicle compatibility can be executed declaratively in SPARQL. Future work will incorporate OWL reasoning rules for automated adapter-based compatibility inference and link the triplestore endpoint directly to a web-based mapping and navigation interface.

